# Lemon WMS — Cursor Agent Rules

These rules govern every file you create or modify in this codebase.
Read ARCHITECTURE.md and DATA-LAYER.md before writing any code.
These rules assume you have read both.

---

## Before you write any code

1. Read ARCHITECTURE.md if you have not already.
2. Read DATA-LAYER.md if you have not already.
3. Identify which domain the work belongs to. Every file belongs to exactly one domain.
4. Identify which layer (lib, api, components) the work belongs to.
5. Check that you are not crossing the client/server boundary.

---

## Hard stops — ask before proceeding

Do not proceed silently. Stop and ask the developer when:

- A new database migration is required (schema change, new model, new column, new index).
- A new npm dependency needs to be installed.
- A file needs to move from one folder to another.
- A domain boundary is unclear and two domains could plausibly own a piece of logic.
- A business rule is ambiguous and the wrong interpretation would require a rewrite.

These are not interruptions. Getting them wrong is far more expensive than asking.

---

## Domain rules

Every file lives in exactly one domain. The domain names are:
`iam`, `locations`, `parties`, `catalog`, `stock`, `orders`, `logs`, `system`

These names are used consistently in:
- `prisma/schema/{domain}.prisma`
- `lib/{domain}/{file}.ts`
- `app/api/` — resource-named routes (NOT domain-named — see API rules)
- `components/dashboard/{domain}/` and `components/warehouse/{domain}/`

If you cannot assign a file to a domain without hesitation, flag it before creating it.

---

## Layer rules

### `lib/` — server only

- Contains Prisma queries and business logic.
- Never imported by anything in `components/` or by client-side code.
- One file per model or logical grouping within the domain.
- Functions have explicit TypeScript return types — always.
- Functions never return raw Prisma objects — always map to a typed response shape.

### `app/api/` — the boundary

- Route handlers do exactly three things: `withAuth` → Zod parse → call `lib/` function.
- No business logic in route handlers. None. Not even "simple" validation beyond Zod.
- No Prisma calls in route handlers. Not even a `findUnique`. That goes in `lib/`.
- Every response uses the `ApiResponse<T>` envelope — see DATA-LAYER.md.
- URL structure reflects resource hierarchy, not internal domain grouping.
  - Correct: `/api/warehouses`, `/api/zones/:id/bins`, `/api/orders/purchase`
  - Wrong: `/api/locations/warehouses`, `/api/iam/users`

### `components/` — client only

- Hooks live next to the component that uses them — same folder.
- A hook is promoted to a shared location only when it is genuinely used from
  two or more separate component locations. Do not pre-promote.
- Types that are only used within a component cluster live in a `types.ts` file
  in that component folder — not in `src/types/`.
- `src/types/` is for types that genuinely cross the client/server boundary:
  API response envelopes, the JWT payload shape, shared enums.

---

## Data fetching rules

This codebase does not use React Query. Server state is managed with custom hooks
using `useState` + `useEffect` + Axios. Read DATA-LAYER.md for the full contract.

The short version:

- Every data hook exposes `isLoading`, `error`, and `refetch`. Always. No exceptions.
- Errors are never silently swallowed with `console.error`. They set `error` state.
- Mutations return `{ success: boolean; error?: string }` — they never throw to the caller.
- The order pool and any time-sensitive screen polls on an interval. Set it in the hook.
- Components never call Axios directly. They consume hook return values only.
- Components render three states: loading skeleton, error banner, data. Always all three.

---

## Log layer rules — read carefully

`lib/logs/log.service.ts` is the only file that writes to `UserActivityEntry`,
`BinOperationEntry`, or `ItemLedgerEntry`.

**Never call `prisma.userActivityEntry.create()` from any other file.**
**Never call `prisma.binOperationEntry.create()` from any other file.**
**Never call `prisma.itemLedgerEntry.create()` from any other file.**

Every function in every other service that moves stock must call
`log.service.ts` functions, not Prisma directly.

The three log tables are never updated and never deleted. If a mistake was made,
write a compensating entry with the opposite `quantityDelta`. Both entries stay.

Write order within a transaction: UAE first, then BOE, then ILE.

The ILE↔BOE circular reference is resolved by pre-generating UUIDs for both
before the transaction begins, then inserting both in the same transaction.

---

## Prisma rules

- `select` fields only accept `true` — never a dynamic boolean value.
- Soft deletes only. Use `deletedAt = new Date()`. Never call `.delete()` on any
  model that has a `deletedAt` field.
- Never expose raw Prisma model types in API responses. Define a response type
  and map to it explicitly.
- Schema changes require confirmation before writing a migration.
- All enums live in `prisma/schema/_base.prisma` — never in a domain schema file.

---

## TypeScript rules

- Strict mode. `any` is not permitted. If you think you need `any`, you need
  a more specific type or an unknown with a type guard.
- No type assertions (`as SomeType`) without a comment explaining why the
  type system cannot infer this correctly.
- Functional only. No classes. No `this`.
- Named exports only. Default exports are only used for Next.js page and layout
  components, which require them by convention.
- Explicit return types on every function in `lib/`. Return types on components
  are optional but encouraged.

---

## Component rules

- Props interface declared above the component, not inline.
- One component per file.
- No inline styles. Tailwind classes only.
- Dashboard components use shadcn/ui primitives.
- Floor components use the custom minimal design system — see FLOOR-TERMINAL-AESTHETIC.md.
  Do not use shadcn/ui components in floor-side files. They are not optimised for
  touch or the dark industrial aesthetic.
- Every component that consumes a data hook must handle loading, error, and data
  states explicitly. A component that renders empty defaults while loading is a bug.

---

## Auth rules

- Every API route uses `withAuth(req, allowedRoles)` as the first call.
- Auth routes (`/api/auth/*`) are the only unprotected routes.
- Role checks use the `Role` enum — never compare role strings directly.
- Never log the raw access token or refresh token. Never store them unencrypted.
- PINs are 4-digit numeric strings hashed with bcrypt. Treat them with the same
  care as passwords even though they are short.
- The access token lives in localStorage and the Zustand auth store. The refresh
  token lives in an httpOnly cookie only. Do not move these without explicit instruction.
- Floor context (warehouseId, zoneId, deviceId) is injected as request headers
  by `warehouseApiClient`. Never pass floor context in the request body.

---

## Axios client rules

Three Axios instances exist. Use the correct one for the context:

- `sharedApi` — bearer token only. For routes that need auth but no role context.
- `dashboardApiClient` — bearer token + `x-user-id` + `x-user-role`. Dashboard routes.
- `warehouseApiClient` — bearer token + user headers + `x-warehouse-id` + `x-zone-id`
  + `x-device-id`. All floor routes.

Never call `sharedApi` from a dashboard or floor component when a typed client exists.
Never create a fourth Axios instance without explicit instruction.

---

## Naming conventions

| Thing | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Files | kebab-case | `bin-stock.ts`, `use-warehouse-home.ts` |
| Components | PascalCase | `WarehouseCard.tsx` |
| Hooks | camelCase with `use` prefix | `useWarehouseHome.ts` |
| Service functions | camelCase | `createWarehouse`, `getZoneById` |
| Types / interfaces | PascalCase | `WarehouseResponse`, `CreateBinInput` |
| Zod schemas | camelCase with `Schema` suffix | `createBinSchema` |
| API response fields | camelCase | `{ warehouseId, createdAt }` |

---

## What not to build for MVP

Do not build, scaffold, or stub these unless explicitly instructed:

- Box / BoxLine service logic or API routes (schema only — deferred)
- Email notification sending (in-app Notification model only)
- WebSocket or SSE real-time updates (polling only — interval set in hook)
- CSV import or export endpoints
- PDF generation
- Multi-tenancy logic
- Dark mode toggle
- Audit log export endpoint

If a task description touches any of these, confirm before proceeding.
